{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvwADtUy8eqrpif25GnR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EHkiHZjdsLU4GsJG2buy4d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\n\\newcommand{\\score}{\\mathrm{Score}}\n\n\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nSoftmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors that sum to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage alone says little about whether the sets are reliable for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted test data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result holds only for the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are not available.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMany predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Variants that address one of these questions typically do so at the cost of another \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI}, so comparisons that report only average coverage and average set size can hide the trade-off.\n\nThis paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds three negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n\\emph{does a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step achieve a better composite reliability score than standard split-conformal baselines, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?}\n\nThe answer we can support is conditional. On sealed final data the studied procedure obtains a higher composite score than the best of four evaluator-implemented baselines, with a paired margin whose bootstrap interval excludes zero; it also remains close to nominal marginal coverage on exchangeable cases. At the same time, the sealed metrics show non-trivial residual under-coverage on shifted cases and on worst slices, and the retained artifacts do not allow us to say which baseline was best, which cases drive the residual gaps, or whether the result extends beyond the declared families.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) We write down the three-axis protocol in reader-checkable form, including the loss, the slice definitions, the negative controls and the validity conditions, and derive simple properties relevant to interpreting scores (for example, that the full prediction set scores exactly 0.75 and that the one-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage).\n(ii) We specify one concrete procedure, composed from known pieces (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, a held-out gate), and state which of its properties are exact and which are only approximate when the weights are estimated.\n(iii) We report a sealed final comparison with uncertainty and decompose the residual loss into its marginal, slice and size components.\n(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\nExact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Class-conditional calibration (a form of Mondrian calibration) is a standard remedy for label-dependent miscoverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses its sample-size problem when classes are many or long-tailed \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\nSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal ideas in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n\n\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\nConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied and tutorial treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging, histopathology, pattern recognition and software-tutorial venues \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF,Rueda2007ProgressIP,Löfström2022TutorialFU}. We characterise these works only to the extent of their titles and venues, which is all the retrieval metadata record.\n\n\\paragraph{Positioning.}\nOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\nLet $p(x)\\in\\Delta^{K-1}$ be the softmax output of a fixed $K$-class predictor, with $y\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$. A conformal procedure receives $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ labelled calibration pairs $(p_i,y_i)$, the level $\\alpha$, and $n_{\\mathrm{test}}$ unlabelled test vectors, and returns a set $\\mathcal C(x)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$ for each test vector. The evaluator owns the data: logits are $z=\\epsilon+s\\,d\\,e_y+\\beta\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_K)$, signal strength $s$, a log-normal heteroscedastic multiplier $d$ on the true-class signal, a prior tilt $\\beta$, and temperature scaling before the softmax; the class prior is balanced or long-tailed. Test data may differ from calibration data through the class prior and a signal-strength multiplier (``shifted'' cases); otherwise a case is exchangeable. The declared range is $2\\le K\\le 100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le 2000$, $n_{\\mathrm{test}}\\le 4000$ in the search-time evaluator source; the sealed final case manifest (families, $K$, $\\alpha$, sample sizes) is not retained and is therefore unknown to us.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#gen_split]]\n\n\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\nFor one episode with set indicator $\\mathcal C(x_j)$ on $n$ test points, let $\\mathrm{cov}$ be empirical coverage, $\\rho=\\frac1{nK}\\sum_j|\\mathcal C(x_j)|$ the set-size ratio, and $g_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\max(0,1-\\alpha-\\mathrm{cov})$ the one-sided marginal gap. The worst-slice gap $g_{\\mathrm{s}}$ is the largest one-sided coverage shortfall over slices with at least 30 test points: classes when $K\\le10$, and strata of set size and of maximum probability (quartiles) when $K>10$. The episode loss and score are\n\\begin{equation}\n\\ell=0.5\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm m}/\\alpha)+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm s})+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(\\rho),\\qquad \\score=1-\\overline{\\ell},\n\\label{eq:loss}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\overline\\ell$ averages over seeds within a case and then with equal weight over cases. Two properties follow directly from Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. First, the full set has $g_{\\mathrm m}=g_{\\mathrm s}=0$ and $\\rho=1$, so it scores exactly $0.75$ on every case; any useful procedure must beat this by shrinking sets without opening coverage gaps. Second, the gaps are one-sided: over-coverage is penalised only through $\\rho$, so a small gap does not show that coverage is tight. Shift-case coverage enters $\\score$ through the same terms, but the shift gap is reported as a descriptive metric and is not a validity condition.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#score_sets]]\n\n\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\nThe evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n\nThree negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#control_verdicts]]\n\n\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\nWith exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n\n\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\nIf test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n\n\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\nUnder the normal approximation to the Beta law, calibration-induced coverage is $C\\approx\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2\\approx\\alpha(1-\\alpha)/(n+2)$ and $\\mu\\ge1-\\alpha$. The expected one-sided shortfall is $\\sigma\\,f(\\delta)$ with $f(\\delta)=\\varphi(\\delta)-\\delta(1-\\Phi(\\delta))$ and $\\delta=(\\mu-(1-\\alpha))/\\sigma$. An exact-level quantile has $\\delta\\approx0$ and $f(0)=0.399$, whereas shifting the level by $0.7\\sigma$ gives $f(0.7)=0.143$, about 36\\% of the unmargined value, at the price of larger sets. This is the motivation for the variance-aware margins used below (multipliers 0.7 pooled, 0.6 per group). It is a heuristic derivation about calibration noise only; we have no unmargined ablation, so it is not checked experimentally here.\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]\n\n\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\nThe score of label $k$ is $1-p_k(x)$ (LAC). For $K\\le10$ the Mondrian groups are the labels, because the evaluator's slices are classes; for $K>10$ they are confidence bins of the maximum probability (at most 10 equal-mass bins, at least 80 calibration rows each), a function of $x$ alone. Each group's threshold is the finite-sample conformal quantile at level $\\alpha_g=\\alpha-z\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/n_g+k_g/M)}$ with $z=0.6$ per group and $0.7$ for the pooled threshold, where $M$ is the number of test rows and $k_g$ the number of groups; group thresholds are shrunk toward the pooled threshold with weight $n_g/(n_g+20)$, and groups too small for a finite quantile use the pooled value. The set is $\\{k: 1-p_k(x)\\le q_{g}\\}$.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#mondrian_conformal_quantile]]\n\n\\paragraph{Gated cross-fitted weighting.}\nEach probability vector is mapped to five summary features (logit of the top probability, top-two log ratio, normalised entropy, mean top-five log probability minus the top log probability, log normalised Gini impurity), standardised with calibration moments and clipped to $\\pm8$. As test chunks arrive, a 2-fold cross-fitted ridge-penalised logistic classifier (penalty 1, class-balanced weights) separates calibration from all test rows seen so far, and the held-out likelihood-ratio statistic $\\mathrm{LR}=2(\\ell_{\\mathrm{full}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{null}})$ against an intercept-only model gives a positive-part shrinkage factor $\\beta=\\max(0,1-d/\\mathrm{LR})$ with $d=5$. If fewer than 40 test rows have been seen, or $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$, or any quantity is non-finite, the unweighted Mondrian thresholds are used verbatim. Otherwise weights $w=\\exp(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta z,\\pm\\log 8))$ are built from the centred held-out log-odds $z$, and each test row receives weighted per-group thresholds with its own weight at $+\\infty$ and effective-sample-size margins ($n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w)^2/\\sum w^2$). Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is non-positive in expectation; it is not guaranteed to be inactive on every draw, and its activation frequency was not logged.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#_domain_logweights]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/03_cross_fitted_group_indicator_quantile_regression_conformal_thresholds/program_1/src/program.py#handle]]\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development score of V2 and V3 tie exactly, and V2 is the configuration recorded by the selection manifest.\n\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated once on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} We report the five per-seed final scores, their sample standard deviation, and the evaluator's bootstrap interval for the paired margin. For the three development seeds we report paired per-seed differences without a significance test: with three pairs, the smallest attainable two-sided exact sign-flip $p$-value is $0.25$.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\nThe studied procedure attains a sealed final score of 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:final}, Figure~\\ref{fig:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.best_baseline_score]]\nThe paired margin is 0.0387 with bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499, which excludes zero. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nIn loss terms the mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6\\%. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThe five seeded runs score between 0.812 and 0.862 (mean 0.8416, standard deviation 0.019), so every seeded run is above the aggregate best-baseline score, although this compares individual seeds with an aggregate. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_per_seed]]\nNo episode failed and all three negative controls passed, and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.controls_passed_fraction]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/final_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed final comparison. (a) Paired score margin of the studied procedure over the best of four baselines with its 95\\% bootstrap interval (seeded runs within cases, equal weight over cases). (b) Per-seed final scores of the procedure; horizontal lines mark the procedure's aggregate score and the best-baseline aggregate score, so per-seed dots are compared with an aggregate.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics retained by the evaluator. Gaps are one-sided shortfalls; ``shift'' is descriptive.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nComposite score of studied procedure & 0.8416 \\\\\nComposite score of best baseline (identity not retained) & 0.8029 \\\\\nPaired margin (95\\% bootstrap interval) & 0.0387 (0.0274, 0.0499) \\\\\nExchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0017 \\\\\nWorst-slice under-coverage gap (all cases) & 0.0675 \\\\\nShift-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0536 \\\\\nMean set-size ratio & 0.209 \\\\\nNegative controls passed & 3 of 3 (fraction 1.0) \\\\\nFailed episodes & 0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\nThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.shift_coverage_gap]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap]]\n\nBecause Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss} is linear in the case-mean metrics, the mean loss of 0.1584 decomposes as follows: the set-size term contributes $0.25\\times0.209=0.0522$, the slice term $0.25\\times0.0675=0.0169$, and the remainder, $0.0893$, is the marginal term, equivalent to a mean clipped marginal gap of $0.179$ in units of $\\alpha$ over all cases. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThis subtraction relies on the evaluator aggregating the components exactly as reported, and $\\alpha$ is not retained, so we do not convert it to a coverage number. Since exchangeable-case gaps are small, the marginal term is plausibly dominated by shifted cases, but we cannot establish this without per-case results. The largest single residual cost is therefore marginal under-coverage, not set size. We do not attribute the residual shift or slice gaps to statistical noise, to the density-ratio estimation, or to a validity failure of the weighting: no gate-level diagnostics are retained.\n\n\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\nThe per-seed paired differences in score are 0.064, 0.043 and 0.045, all positive, but with three seeds this is descriptive rather than inferential. Because the components are changed together we can say only that the V2 configuration outperformed V1 on these development cases, not which element of the weighting step is responsible.\n\nV3 reproduced V2 in every retained field: score, all six metrics and all three per-seed scores. The V3 source falls back to the V2 path verbatim when its calibration-only gate does not activate, which would give this outcome; but whether the gate was inactive or the quantile-regression thresholds were exercised and rejected is not recorded, so the cause of the tie remains unknown. We therefore report no evidence for or against quantile-regression thresholds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/variant_profile.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Search-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or over-fitting. Only the sealed numbers should be used for conclusions.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Procedure specification}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} lists the constants of the studied procedure as they appear in its source, and Algorithm-style pseudo-code follows.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#constants]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the studied procedure (from the frozen configuration's source).}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Constant & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nScore & nonconformity & $1-p_k(x)$ \\\\\nMondrian & class-conditional if & $K\\le10$ \\\\\nMondrian & confidence bins ($K>10$): max bins / min rows per bin & 10 / 80 \\\\\nMargin & pooled / per-group multiplier $z$ & 0.7 / 0.6 \\\\\nShrinkage & pseudo-count $n_0$ toward pooled threshold & 20 \\\\\nGate & min. test rows before domain fit & 40 \\\\\nGate & weights used only if $\\beta>$ & $10^{-3}$ \\\\\nDomain classifier & ridge penalty / cross-fit folds / features $d$ & 1 / 2 / 5 \\\\\nWeights & clip range (multiplicative) & $[1/8,\\,8]$ \\\\\nFeatures & standardised clip & $\\pm8$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Pseudo-code.}\n\\emph{Calibrate:} compute scores $s_i=1-p_{y_i}(x_i)$; define groups (label if $K\\le10$, else confidence bin); compute per-group margin thresholds $q_g$ and the pooled threshold, with shrinkage; standardise summary features with calibration moments and assign calibration rows to two folds at random.\n\\emph{Predict (per test chunk):} append the chunk's features; if at least 40 test rows are available, cross-fit the domain classifier on the two folds, compute $\\mathrm{LR}$ and $\\beta$; if $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$ or any quantity is non-finite, return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}\\}$ with unweighted thresholds; else compute weights $w_i$ (calibration) and $w_j$ (test), form for each test row the weighted group thresholds with $w_j$ at $+\\infty$, and return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}(x_j)\\}$. The wire protocol limits calibration to two chunks of at most 1000 rows and test data to four chunks.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nThe search-time evaluator generates data with the logit model of Section~3.1; the sealed final plane uses the same scoring, baseline and bootstrap code with different cases and seeds. Slices require at least 30 test points. Controls: C1 permutes calibration labels; C2 permutes test labels when scoring; C3 replaces all probabilities by the uniform vector. A control episode is discarded as vacuous if fewer than half of the permuted labels changed. The coverage tolerance for exchangeable cases is $3\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/(n_{\\mathrm{cal}}+2)+1/n_{\\mathrm{test}})/n_{\\mathrm{seeds}}}$. The final evaluator source file is not separately retained, so statements about the final plane rely on the plane-conditional branches of the shared evaluator and on the qualification outcome.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-time metrics for the three nested configurations (seeds 101--103; lower is better for gaps and size ratio, higher for score). Controls passed fraction was 1.0 and failed episodes 0 for all three.}\n\\label{tab:dev}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfig. & Score & Exch. gap & Worst-slice gap & Shift gap & Size ratio \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 Mondrian LAC & 0.8683 & 0.00028 & 0.0497 & 0.0613 & 0.166 \\\\\nV2 + gated weighting & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\nV3 + QR thresholds & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-seed scores. Final seeds are sealed-evaluation runs (duration in seconds includes control episodes); development seeds are for V1/V2 (V3 identical to V2).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Seed (dev.) & V1 / V2 score \\\\\n\\midrule\n9001 & 0.8121 (7.6 s) & 101 & 0.8519 / 0.9163 \\\\\n9002 & 0.8495 (4.3 s) & 102 & 0.8547 / 0.8980 \\\\\n9003 & 0.8478 (4.3 s) & 103 & 0.8984 / 0.9433 \\\\\n9004 & 0.8620 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n9005 & 0.8365 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Full-set score: $1-(0.5\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot1)=0.75$ for every case (analytic).\n\\item Relative loss reduction: $(0.1971-0.1584)/0.1971=19.6\\%$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\n\\item Expected one-sided shortfall factor: $f(0)=0.399$, $f(0.6)=0.169$, $f(0.7)=0.143$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]\n\\item Development paired differences V2$-$V1: 0.064, 0.043, 0.045 (mean 0.051); V3$-$V2: 0, 0, 0.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\nThe figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~2 rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only; several standard references on split-conformal theory, covariate-shift weighting and compositional data analysis are not in the retrieved bibliography and are therefore stated as background without citation.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used in this work in two roles. First, an LLM-driven research agent proposed, implemented and evaluated three candidate configurations (V1--V3 here) against the evaluator and wrote the initial analysis. Second, an LLM drafted this manuscript from the evaluator outputs and the retained sources. All reported numbers are copied or arithmetically derived from evaluator outputs; the human-facing responsibility for claims and for the choice of venue framing rests with the authors of record.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2115,"cache_read_input_tokens":155213,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2115},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163efe90-675b-4e09-a080-713d4bfbbf15","uuid":"d6d0091e-9bc3-49f9-8e53-1a7f4a7ad53c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:53:10.618Z","request_id":"req_011CgvwABzsqAr7D8e4qvQu1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EHkiHZjdsLU4GsJG2buy4d","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /workspace/paper/paper.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163efe90-675b-4e09-a080-713d4bfbbf15","uuid":"9622dfac-f440-4124-a173-35fee41a39fa","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:53:10.648Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\n\\newcommand{\\score}{\\mathrm{Score}}\n\n\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nSoftmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors that sum to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage alone says little about whether the sets are reliable for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted test data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result holds only for the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are not available.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMany predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Variants that address one of these questions typically do so at the cost of another \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI}, so comparisons that report only average coverage and average set size can hide the trade-off.\n\nThis paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds three negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n\\emph{does a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step achieve a better composite reliability score than standard split-conformal baselines, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?}\n\nThe answer we can support is conditional. On sealed final data the studied procedure obtains a higher composite score than the best of four evaluator-implemented baselines, with a paired margin whose bootstrap interval excludes zero; it also remains close to nominal marginal coverage on exchangeable cases. At the same time, the sealed metrics show non-trivial residual under-coverage on shifted cases and on worst slices, and the retained artifacts do not allow us to say which baseline was best, which cases drive the residual gaps, or whether the result extends beyond the declared families.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) We write down the three-axis protocol in reader-checkable form, including the loss, the slice definitions, the negative controls and the validity conditions, and derive simple properties relevant to interpreting scores (for example, that the full prediction set scores exactly 0.75 and that the one-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage).\n(ii) We specify one concrete procedure, composed from known pieces (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, a held-out gate), and state which of its properties are exact and which are only approximate when the weights are estimated.\n(iii) We report a sealed final comparison with uncertainty and decompose the residual loss into its marginal, slice and size components.\n(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\nExact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Class-conditional calibration (a form of Mondrian calibration) is a standard remedy for label-dependent miscoverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses its sample-size problem when classes are many or long-tailed \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\nSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal ideas in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n\n\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\nConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied and tutorial treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging, histopathology, pattern recognition and software-tutorial venues \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF,Rueda2007ProgressIP,Löfström2022TutorialFU}. We characterise these works only to the extent of their titles and venues, which is all the retrieval metadata record.\n\n\\paragraph{Positioning.}\nOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\nLet $p(x)\\in\\Delta^{K-1}$ be the softmax output of a fixed $K$-class predictor, with $y\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$. A conformal procedure receives $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ labelled calibration pairs $(p_i,y_i)$, the level $\\alpha$, and $n_{\\mathrm{test}}$ unlabelled test vectors, and returns a set $\\mathcal C(x)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$ for each test vector. The evaluator owns the data: logits are $z=\\epsilon+s\\,d\\,e_y+\\beta\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_K)$, signal strength $s$, a log-normal heteroscedastic multiplier $d$ on the true-class signal, a prior tilt $\\beta$, and temperature scaling before the softmax; the class prior is balanced or long-tailed. Test data may differ from calibration data through the class prior and a signal-strength multiplier (``shifted'' cases); otherwise a case is exchangeable. The declared range is $2\\le K\\le 100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le 2000$, $n_{\\mathrm{test}}\\le 4000$ in the search-time evaluator source; the sealed final case manifest (families, $K$, $\\alpha$, sample sizes) is not retained and is therefore unknown to us.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#gen_split]]\n\n\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\nFor one episode with set indicator $\\mathcal C(x_j)$ on $n$ test points, let $\\mathrm{cov}$ be empirical coverage, $\\rho=\\frac1{nK}\\sum_j|\\mathcal C(x_j)|$ the set-size ratio, and $g_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\max(0,1-\\alpha-\\mathrm{cov})$ the one-sided marginal gap. The worst-slice gap $g_{\\mathrm{s}}$ is the largest one-sided coverage shortfall over slices with at least 30 test points: classes when $K\\le10$, and strata of set size and of maximum probability (quartiles) when $K>10$. The episode loss and score are\n\\begin{equation}\n\\ell=0.5\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm m}/\\alpha)+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g_{\\mathrm s})+0.25\\,\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(\\rho),\\qquad \\score=1-\\overline{\\ell},\n\\label{eq:loss}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\overline\\ell$ averages over seeds within a case and then with equal weight over cases. Two properties follow directly from Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. First, the full set has $g_{\\mathrm m}=g_{\\mathrm s}=0$ and $\\rho=1$, so it scores exactly $0.75$ on every case; any useful procedure must beat this by shrinking sets without opening coverage gaps. Second, the gaps are one-sided: over-coverage is penalised only through $\\rho$, so a small gap does not show that coverage is tight. Shift-case coverage enters $\\score$ through the same terms, but the shift gap is reported as a descriptive metric and is not a validity condition.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#score_sets]]\n\n\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\nThe evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n\nThree negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#control_verdicts]]\n\n\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\nWith exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n\n\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\nIf test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n\n\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\nUnder the normal approximation to the Beta law, calibration-induced coverage is $C\\approx\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2\\approx\\alpha(1-\\alpha)/(n+2)$ and $\\mu\\ge1-\\alpha$. The expected one-sided shortfall is $\\sigma\\,f(\\delta)$ with $f(\\delta)=\\varphi(\\delta)-\\delta(1-\\Phi(\\delta))$ and $\\delta=(\\mu-(1-\\alpha))/\\sigma$. An exact-level quantile has $\\delta\\approx0$ and $f(0)=0.399$, whereas shifting the level by $0.7\\sigma$ gives $f(0.7)=0.143$, about 36\\% of the unmargined value, at the price of larger sets. This is the motivation for the variance-aware margins used below (multipliers 0.7 pooled, 0.6 per group). It is a heuristic derivation about calibration noise only; we have no unmargined ablation, so it is not checked experimentally here.\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]\n\n\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\nThe score of label $k$ is $1-p_k(x)$ (LAC). For $K\\le10$ the Mondrian groups are the labels, because the evaluator's slices are classes; for $K>10$ they are confidence bins of the maximum probability (at most 10 equal-mass bins, at least 80 calibration rows each), a function of $x$ alone. Each group's threshold is the finite-sample conformal quantile at level $\\alpha_g=\\alpha-z\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/n_g+k_g/M)}$ with $z=0.6$ per group and $0.7$ for the pooled threshold, where $M$ is the number of test rows and $k_g$ the number of groups; group thresholds are shrunk toward the pooled threshold with weight $n_g/(n_g+20)$, and groups too small for a finite quantile use the pooled value. The set is $\\{k: 1-p_k(x)\\le q_{g}\\}$.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#mondrian_conformal_quantile]]\n\n\\paragraph{Gated cross-fitted weighting.}\nEach probability vector is mapped to five summary features (logit of the top probability, top-two log ratio, normalised entropy, mean top-five log probability minus the top log probability, log normalised Gini impurity), standardised with calibration moments and clipped to $\\pm8$. As test chunks arrive, a 2-fold cross-fitted ridge-penalised logistic classifier (penalty 1, class-balanced weights) separates calibration from all test rows seen so far, and the held-out likelihood-ratio statistic $\\mathrm{LR}=2(\\ell_{\\mathrm{full}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{null}})$ against an intercept-only model gives a positive-part shrinkage factor $\\beta=\\max(0,1-d/\\mathrm{LR})$ with $d=5$. If fewer than 40 test rows have been seen, or $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$, or any quantity is non-finite, the unweighted Mondrian thresholds are used verbatim. Otherwise weights $w=\\exp(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta z,\\pm\\log 8))$ are built from the centred held-out log-odds $z$, and each test row receives weighted per-group thresholds with its own weight at $+\\infty$ and effective-sample-size margins ($n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w)^2/\\sum w^2$). Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is non-positive in expectation; it is not guaranteed to be inactive on every draw, and its activation frequency was not logged.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#_domain_logweights]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/03_cross_fitted_group_indicator_quantile_regression_conformal_thresholds/program_1/src/program.py#handle]]\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development score of V2 and V3 tie exactly, and V2 is the configuration recorded by the selection manifest.\n\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated once on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} We report the five per-seed final scores, their sample standard deviation, and the evaluator's bootstrap interval for the paired margin. For the three development seeds we report paired per-seed differences without a significance test: with three pairs, the smallest attainable two-sided exact sign-flip $p$-value is $0.25$.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\nThe studied procedure attains a sealed final score of 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:final}, Figure~\\ref{fig:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.best_baseline_score]]\nThe paired margin is 0.0387 with bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499, which excludes zero. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nIn loss terms the mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6\\%. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThe five seeded runs score between 0.812 and 0.862 (mean 0.8416, standard deviation 0.019), so every seeded run is above the aggregate best-baseline score, although this compares individual seeds with an aggregate. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_per_seed]]\nNo episode failed and all three negative controls passed, and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.controls_passed_fraction]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/final_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed final comparison. (a) Paired score margin of the studied procedure over the best of four baselines with its 95\\% bootstrap interval (seeded runs within cases, equal weight over cases). (b) Per-seed final scores of the procedure; horizontal lines mark the procedure's aggregate score and the best-baseline aggregate score, so per-seed dots are compared with an aggregate.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics retained by the evaluator. Gaps are one-sided shortfalls; ``shift'' is descriptive.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nComposite score of studied procedure & 0.8416 \\\\\nComposite score of best baseline (identity not retained) & 0.8029 \\\\\nPaired margin (95\\% bootstrap interval) & 0.0387 (0.0274, 0.0499) \\\\\nExchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0017 \\\\\nWorst-slice under-coverage gap (all cases) & 0.0675 \\\\\nShift-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0536 \\\\\nMean set-size ratio & 0.209 \\\\\nNegative controls passed & 3 of 3 (fraction 1.0) \\\\\nFailed episodes & 0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\nThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.shift_coverage_gap]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap]]\n\nBecause Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss} is linear in the case-mean metrics, the mean loss of 0.1584 decomposes as follows: the set-size term contributes $0.25\\times0.209=0.0522$, the slice term $0.25\\times0.0675=0.0169$, and the remainder, $0.0893$, is the marginal term, equivalent to a mean clipped marginal gap of $0.179$ in units of $\\alpha$ over all cases. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThis subtraction relies on the evaluator aggregating the components exactly as reported, and $\\alpha$ is not retained, so we do not convert it to a coverage number. Since exchangeable-case gaps are small, the marginal term is plausibly dominated by shifted cases, but we cannot establish this without per-case results. The largest single residual cost is therefore marginal under-coverage, not set size. We do not attribute the residual shift or slice gaps to statistical noise, to the density-ratio estimation, or to a validity failure of the weighting: no gate-level diagnostics are retained.\n\n\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\nThe per-seed paired differences in score are 0.064, 0.043 and 0.045, all positive, but with three seeds this is descriptive rather than inferential. Because the components are changed together we can say only that the V2 configuration outperformed V1 on these development cases, not which element of the weighting step is responsible.\n\nV3 reproduced V2 in every retained field: score, all six metrics and all three per-seed scores. The V3 source falls back to the V2 path verbatim when its calibration-only gate does not activate, which would give this outcome; but whether the gate was inactive or the quantile-regression thresholds were exercised and rejected is not recorded, so the cause of the tie remains unknown. We therefore report no evidence for or against quantile-regression thresholds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/variant_profile.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Search-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or over-fitting. Only the sealed numbers should be used for conclusions.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Procedure specification}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} lists the constants of the studied procedure as they appear in its source, and Algorithm-style pseudo-code follows.\n% @evidence[[results/ideas/02_gated_domain_classifier_weighted_mondrian_conformal/program_1/src/program.py#constants]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the studied procedure (from the frozen configuration's source).}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Constant & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nScore & nonconformity & $1-p_k(x)$ \\\\\nMondrian & class-conditional if & $K\\le10$ \\\\\nMondrian & confidence bins ($K>10$): max bins / min rows per bin & 10 / 80 \\\\\nMargin & pooled / per-group multiplier $z$ & 0.7 / 0.6 \\\\\nShrinkage & pseudo-count $n_0$ toward pooled threshold & 20 \\\\\nGate & min. test rows before domain fit & 40 \\\\\nGate & weights used only if $\\beta>$ & $10^{-3}$ \\\\\nDomain classifier & ridge penalty / cross-fit folds / features $d$ & 1 / 2 / 5 \\\\\nWeights & clip range (multiplicative) & $[1/8,\\,8]$ \\\\\nFeatures & standardised clip & $\\pm8$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Pseudo-code.}\n\\emph{Calibrate:} compute scores $s_i=1-p_{y_i}(x_i)$; define groups (label if $K\\le10$, else confidence bin); compute per-group margin thresholds $q_g$ and the pooled threshold, with shrinkage; standardise summary features with calibration moments and assign calibration rows to two folds at random.\n\\emph{Predict (per test chunk):} append the chunk's features; if at least 40 test rows are available, cross-fit the domain classifier on the two folds, compute $\\mathrm{LR}$ and $\\beta$; if $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$ or any quantity is non-finite, return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}\\}$ with unweighted thresholds; else compute weights $w_i$ (calibration) and $w_j$ (test), form for each test row the weighted group thresholds with $w_j$ at $+\\infty$, and return $\\{k:1-p_k\\le q_{g}(x_j)\\}$. The wire protocol limits calibration to two chunks of at most 1000 rows and test data to four chunks.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nThe search-time evaluator generates data with the logit model of Section~3.1; the sealed final plane uses the same scoring, baseline and bootstrap code with different cases and seeds. Slices require at least 30 test points. Controls: C1 permutes calibration labels; C2 permutes test labels when scoring; C3 replaces all probabilities by the uniform vector. A control episode is discarded as vacuous if fewer than half of the permuted labels changed. The coverage tolerance for exchangeable cases is $3\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/(n_{\\mathrm{cal}}+2)+1/n_{\\mathrm{test}})/n_{\\mathrm{seeds}}}$. The final evaluator source file is not separately retained, so statements about the final plane rely on the plane-conditional branches of the shared evaluator and on the qualification outcome.\n% @evidence[[results/task/evaluate.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-time metrics for the three nested configurations (seeds 101--103; lower is better for gaps and size ratio, higher for score). Controls passed fraction was 1.0 and failed episodes 0 for all three.}\n\\label{tab:dev}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfig. & Score & Exch. gap & Worst-slice gap & Shift gap & Size ratio \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 Mondrian LAC & 0.8683 & 0.00028 & 0.0497 & 0.0613 & 0.166 \\\\\nV2 + gated weighting & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\nV3 + QR thresholds & 0.9192 & 0.00028 & 0.0376 & 0.0201 & 0.181 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-seed scores. Final seeds are sealed-evaluation runs (duration in seconds includes control episodes); development seeds are for V1/V2 (V3 identical to V2).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Seed (dev.) & V1 / V2 score \\\\\n\\midrule\n9001 & 0.8121 (7.6 s) & 101 & 0.8519 / 0.9163 \\\\\n9002 & 0.8495 (4.3 s) & 102 & 0.8547 / 0.8980 \\\\\n9003 & 0.8478 (4.3 s) & 103 & 0.8984 / 0.9433 \\\\\n9004 & 0.8620 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n9005 & 0.8365 (4.3 s) & & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Full-set score: $1-(0.5\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot0+0.25\\cdot1)=0.75$ for every case (analytic).\n\\item Relative loss reduction: $(0.1971-0.1584)/0.1971=19.6\\%$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\n\\item Expected one-sided shortfall factor: $f(0)=0.399$, $f(0.6)=0.169$, $f(0.7)=0.143$. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]\n\\item Development paired differences V2$-$V1: 0.064, 0.043, 0.045 (mean 0.051); V3$-$V2: 0, 0, 0.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\nThe figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~2 rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only; several standard references on split-conformal theory, covariate-shift weighting and compositional data analysis are not in the retrieved bibliography and are therefore stated as background without citation.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used in this work in two roles. First, an LLM-driven research agent proposed, implemented and evaluated three candidate configurations (V1--V3 here) against the evaluator and wrote the initial analysis. Second, an LLM drafted this manuscript from the evaluator outputs and the retained sources. All reported numbers are copied or arithmetically derived from evaluator outputs; the human-facing responsibility for claims and for the choice of venue framing rests with the authors of record.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
