{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvwHFsF8mRx5n77kqLYy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EQ9XdEHNTS9yn2KyrLu3Qo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'; t=open(p).read()\nR=[\n(\"Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is non-positive in expectation; it is not guaranteed\",\"Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is typically non-positive; it is not guaranteed\"),\n(\"Class-conditional calibration (a form of Mondrian calibration) is a standard remedy for label-dependent miscoverage \\\\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses its sample-size problem when classes are many or long-tailed \\\\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}.\",\"Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\\\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\\\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}.\"),\n(\"\\\\subsection{Search-to-final difference}\",\"\\\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\"),\n(\" All reported numbers are copied or arithmetically derived from evaluator outputs; the human-facing responsibility for claims and for the choice of venue framing rests with the authors of record.\",\" All reported numbers are copied or arithmetically derived from evaluator outputs.\"),\n(\"Section~3.1\",\"Section~\\\\ref{sec:setup}\"),(\"Section~2 rely\",\"Section~\\\\ref{sec:related} rely\"),\n(\"\\\\section{Related Work}\",\"\\\\section{Related Work}\\\\label{sec:related}\"),\n(\"\\\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\",\"\\\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\\\label{sec:setup}\"),\n(\"Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors that sum to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage alone says little about whether the sets are reliable for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted test data. We study\",\"Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study\"),\n(\"The result holds only for the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are not available.\",\"The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in R:\n    assert a in t,a[:50]; t=t.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n -i -w \"idea\\|ideas\\|program\\|programs\\|attempt\\|attempts\\|eligible\\|rubric\\|phase\\|stage\\|round\\|milestone\\|progression\\|timeline\\|then\" paper.tex | grep -v \"^.*@evidence\" | head; bash .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex 2>&1 | tail -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14800,"cache_read_input_tokens":157328,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14800},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163efe90-675b-4e09-a080-713d4bfbbf15","uuid":"4b2fe221-a1ff-4890-93bd-cf434024b63e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:53:31.139Z","request_id":"req_011CgvwHDtvHB3GiWwSXmWNC"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQ9XdEHNTS9yn2KyrLu3Qo","type":"tool_result","content":"65:Set size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal ideas in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n84:where $\\overline\\ell$ averages over seeds within a case and then with equal weight over cases. Two properties follow directly from Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. First, the full set has $g_{\\mathrm m}=g_{\\mathrm s}=0$ and $\\rho=1$, so it scores exactly $0.75$ on every case; any useful procedure must beat this by shrinking sets without opening coverage gaps. Second, the gaps are one-sided: over-coverage is penalised only through $\\rho$, so a small gap does not show that coverage is tight. Shift-case coverage enters $\\score$ through the same terms, but the shift gap is reported as a descriptive metric and is not a validity condition.\nMissing required command: pdftotext","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163efe90-675b-4e09-a080-713d4bfbbf15","uuid":"ea91a7f2-5822-4821-a362-8705c4aee440","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:53:31.204Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"65:Set size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal ideas in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n84:where $\\overline\\ell$ averages over seeds within a case and then with equal weight over cases. Two properties follow directly from Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. First, the full set has $g_{\\mathrm m}=g_{\\mathrm s}=0$ and $\\rho=1$, so it scores exactly $0.75$ on every case; any useful procedure must beat this by shrinking sets without opening coverage gaps. Second, the gaps are one-sided: over-coverage is penalised only through $\\rho$, so a small gap does not show that coverage is tight. Shift-case coverage enters $\\score$ through the same terms, but the shift gap is reported as a descriptive metric and is not a validity condition.\nMissing required command: pdftotext","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
